1095
00:49:01,620 --> 00:49:04,620
The municipal debt has a lower yield in the market.

1096
00:49:04,620 --> 00:49:08,120
Because everybody knows it's not subject to

1097
00:49:08,120 --> 00:49:11,620
income taxes, so it has a low yield.

1098
00:49:11,620 --> 00:49:14,120
So, Yale could issue that debt and use

1099
00:49:14,120 --> 00:49:16,620
the money to invest in high-yielding things that

1100
00:49:16,620 --> 00:49:18,620
are taxable.

1101
00:49:18,620 --> 00:49:20,620
And it's not going to pay taxes on them, either.

1102
00:49:20,620 --> 00:49:22,620
So, there's an arbitrage game that Yale

1103
00:49:22,620 --> 00:49:24,620
could play, or any nonprofit could play.

1104
00:49:24,620 --> 00:49:28,620
But it does not, because it's not

1105
00:49:28,620 --> 00:49:32,620
allowed to, unless it's investing, unless it's using

1106
00:49:32,620 --> 00:49:36,620
the proceeds of the debt for appropriate causes.

1107
00:49:36,620 --> 00:49:40,620
So, you have to understand, this is why

1108
00:49:40,620 --> 00:49:44,620
Yale University has a substantial debt, and why

1109
00:49:44,620 --> 00:49:46,620
Haven has a substantial debt.

1110
00:49:46,620 --> 00:49:58,620
It all makes sense in some basic finance framework.

1111
00:49:58,620 --> 00:50:01,620
But there's a lot of concern about

1112
00:50:01,620 --> 00:50:04,620
indebtedness right now, because with the financial crisis,

1113
00:50:04,620 --> 00:50:07,620
the U.S. and Europe and many other

1114
00:50:07,620 --> 00:50:10,620
countries are suffering debt crises right now.

1115
00:50:10,620 --> 00:50:14,620
So, it's renewing calls for a balanced budget amendment.

1116
00:50:14,620 --> 00:50:17,120
The U.S. government does not have a

1117
00:50:17,120 --> 00:50:19,620
balanced budget amendment like the state governments

1118
00:50:19,620 --> 00:50:20,620
do.

1119
00:50:20,620 --> 00:50:23,620
And in fact, the U.S. government has only one budget.

1120
00:50:23,620 --> 00:50:25,620
It does not have a capital budget.

1121
00:50:25,620 --> 00:50:28,120
So, in some sense, the state governments

1122
00:50:28,120 --> 00:50:30,620
are more sophisticated than the federal government

1123
00:50:30,620 --> 00:50:35,620
in the U.S., in that they have

1124
00:50:35,620 --> 00:50:40,620
the distinction between operating and capital budgets.

1125
00:50:40,620 --> 00:50:42,620
But I guess the reason why the U.S.

1126
00:50:42,620 --> 00:50:43,620
The U.S.

1127
00:50:43,620 --> 00:50:44,620
government has considered the U.S.

1128
00:50:44,620 --> 00:50:46,620
government as adopting a capital budget,

1129
00:50:46,620 --> 00:50:48,620
but it has never done so.

1130
00:50:48,620 --> 00:50:50,620
I think maybe it's because the U.S.

1131
00:50:50,620 --> 00:50:55,620
government has fewer investments, like schools

1132
00:50:55,620 --> 00:51:00,620
or parks or other water facilities, sewage

1133
00:51:00,620 --> 00:51:01,620
facilities.

1134
00:51:01,620 --> 00:51:03,620
It doesn't do that kind of thing.

1135
00:51:03,620 --> 00:51:11,620
So, it doesn't have as many clearly capital projects.

1136
00:51:11,620 --> 00:51:12,620
It has a much bigger debt

1137
00:51:12,620 --> 00:51:13,620
than the state and local governments.

1138
00:51:13,620 --> 00:51:14,620
Okay.

1139
00:51:14,620 --> 00:51:21,620
So, I guess then I'll move to my

1140
00:51:21,620 --> 00:51:28,620
last topic today, which is about government social insurance.

1141
00:51:34,620 --> 00:51:37,620
And this is along the same theme.

1142
00:51:37,620 --> 00:51:39,620
I'm talking today about the

1143
00:51:39,620 --> 00:51:41,620
roles of a financial institution.

1144
00:51:41,620 --> 00:51:48,120
The roles of finance is about

1145
00:51:48,120 --> 00:51:54,620
risk management, and it's about incentivization.

1146
00:51:54,620 --> 00:51:58,620
Some of the basic roles are classified under

1147
00:51:58,620 --> 00:52:02,620
the rubric of social insurance, which is offered

1148
00:52:02,620 --> 00:52:08,620
by the government.

1149
00:52:08,620 --> 00:52:09,620
And this is a huge topic.

1150
00:52:09,620 --> 00:52:10,620
And let me just say this.

1151
00:52:10,620 --> 00:52:18,620
Social insurance refers to insurance that is

1152
00:52:18,620 --> 00:52:26,620
not available by a private insurance company,

1153
00:52:26,620 --> 00:52:32,620
but that are offered generally by governments.

1154
00:52:32,620 --> 00:52:34,620
And let me just give some examples.

1155
00:52:34,620 --> 00:52:38,620
I put in first, progressive taxes.

1156
00:52:38,620 --> 00:52:39,620
Okay.

1157
00:52:39,620 --> 00:52:46,120
So, progressive taxes are income taxes that

1158
00:52:46,120 --> 00:52:52,620
tax higher income people at a lower rate.

1159
00:52:52,620 --> 00:52:57,120
And progressive taxes have increasingly,

1160
00:52:57,120 --> 00:53:01,620
in recent years, adopted something analogous

1161
00:53:01,620 --> 00:53:05,620
to the Earned Income Tax Credit, the EITC.

1162
00:53:05,620 --> 00:53:07,620
That's the U.S. name for it.

1163
00:53:07,620 --> 00:53:10,120
And it's now in many countries around

1164
00:53:10,120 --> 00:53:12,620
the world that provides negative taxes for the

1165
00:53:12,620 --> 00:53:15,620
lowest income people.

1166
00:53:15,620 --> 00:53:21,120
So, the effect of progressive taxes with Earned

1167
00:53:21,120 --> 00:53:26,620
Income Tax Credit is to insulate people somewhat

1168
00:53:26,620 --> 00:53:31,620
from shocks to their income.

1169
00:53:31,620 --> 00:53:34,120
They call it an Earned Income Tax Credit,

1170
00:53:34,120 --> 00:53:36,620
by the way, because you have to have earned

1171
00:53:36,620 --> 00:53:38,620
income to get the credit.

1172
00:53:38,620 --> 00:53:40,620
You can't be just unemployed.

1173
00:53:40,620 --> 00:53:43,620
But if you are working and earning very

1174
00:53:43,620 --> 00:53:46,620
little, especially if you have a family, then you

1175
00:53:46,620 --> 00:53:50,620
have a negative income tax rate, so that

1176
00:53:50,620 --> 00:53:54,620
the government augments your low income with a

1177
00:53:54,620 --> 00:53:56,620
negative tax.

1178
00:53:56,620 --> 00:54:01,120
I think this is very

1179
00:54:01,120 --> 00:54:05,620
important, and it actually is effective.

1180
00:54:05,620 --> 00:54:08,620
Otherwise, the world would be much

1181
00:54:08,620 --> 00:54:11,620
more unequal than it is now.

1182
00:54:11,620 --> 00:54:15,620
I would add, by the way, public services,

1183
00:54:15,620 --> 00:54:19,620
which are offered out of taxes to everyone

1184
00:54:19,620 --> 00:54:23,620
in a country, notably education.

1185
00:54:23,620 --> 00:54:26,620
School systems are generally free.

1186
00:54:26,620 --> 00:54:30,120
And that is, again, something that these are

1187
00:54:30,120 --> 00:54:33,620
all, I consider these a form of insurance,

1188
00:54:33,620 --> 00:54:35,620
because they benefit people.

1189
00:54:35,620 --> 00:54:39,620
They benefit people who are unsuccessful in earning money.

1190
00:54:39,620 --> 00:54:43,620
So, it's like an insurance program against

1191
00:54:43,620 --> 00:54:47,620
possible failure in achieving status in the

1192
00:54:47,620 --> 00:54:51,620
economic system.

1193
00:54:51,620 --> 00:54:56,620
So, we also have, I'll

1194
00:54:56,620 --> 00:55:01,620
go up here, three, Social Security.

1195
00:55:01,620 --> 00:55:03,620
I'm using U.S. terms.

1196
00:55:03,620 --> 00:55:07,620
These are terms that, these things

1197
00:55:07,620 --> 00:55:11,620
happen in virtually all advanced countries.

1198
00:55:11,620 --> 00:55:15,620
In the U.S., we call it OASD.

1199
00:55:15,620 --> 00:55:31,620
That stands for Old Age Survivors and Disability Insurance.

1200
00:55:31,620 --> 00:55:35,620
Old Age Insurance is pensions.

1201
00:55:35,620 --> 00:55:38,120
And that's the biggest part of the

1202
00:55:38,120 --> 00:55:40,620
Social Security system in the United States.

1203
00:55:40,620 --> 00:55:42,620
It's insuring you again.

1204
00:55:42,620 --> 00:55:44,620
Well, it's really something you know is going to happen.

1205
00:55:44,620 --> 00:55:46,620
You're going to get old someday.

1206
00:55:46,620 --> 00:55:48,620
But we call it insurance.

1207
00:55:48,620 --> 00:55:53,620
Survivors insurance is life insurance.

1208
00:55:53,620 --> 00:55:55,620
I mean, it benefits you.

1209
00:55:55,620 --> 00:55:57,620
You're getting too old for this now, but if your

1210
00:55:57,620 --> 00:55:59,620
parents die when you're young, they're going to get old.

1211
00:55:59,620 --> 00:56:00,620
So, it's a great insurance.

1212
00:56:00,620 --> 00:56:02,620
It's a great income.

1213
00:56:02,620 --> 00:56:06,620
The U.S. government will give you an income.

1214
00:56:06,620 --> 00:56:07,620
You're an orphan.

1215
00:56:07,620 --> 00:56:08,620
You have nothing.

1216
00:56:08,620 --> 00:56:11,620
Your parents died and left you nothing.

1217
00:56:11,620 --> 00:56:13,620
You get Survivors Insurance.

1218
00:56:13,620 --> 00:56:15,620
You didn't know you had this, right?

1219
00:56:15,620 --> 00:56:19,620
It's not publicized a lot, but it's part of

1220
00:56:19,620 --> 00:56:23,620
what protects people, and it's offered by the government.

1221
00:56:23,620 --> 00:56:25,620
And then thirdly, Disability Insurance.

1222
00:56:25,620 --> 00:56:28,620
Again, offered by the government.

1223
00:56:28,620 --> 00:56:33,620
It's not against you breaking your neck and becoming paralyzed, or

1224
00:56:33,620 --> 00:56:38,620
anything like that, that makes it impossible for you to work.

1225
00:56:38,620 --> 00:56:42,620
The U.S. government will give you an income for life.

1226
00:56:42,620 --> 00:56:44,620
So, if you are permanently paralyzed,

1227
00:56:44,620 --> 00:56:46,620
you get an income for life.

1228
00:56:46,620 --> 00:56:50,620
All three of these are purchasable in the private sector.

1229
00:56:50,620 --> 00:56:52,620
You can buy a pension.

1230
00:56:52,620 --> 00:56:55,620
You can buy Survivors Insurance.

1231
00:56:55,620 --> 00:56:56,620
They don't call that.

1232
00:56:56,620 --> 00:56:57,620
They call it life insurance.

1233
00:56:57,620 --> 00:57:01,620
It's just turning it around to a different name.

1234
00:57:01,620 --> 00:57:04,620
The sellers of life insurance don't want to remind you

1235
00:57:04,620 --> 00:57:07,620
that you already have a life insurance policy from the government.

1236
00:57:07,620 --> 00:57:10,620
Because you might say, well, I got enough already.

1237
00:57:10,620 --> 00:57:13,620
So, they give it a different name, and

1238
00:57:13,620 --> 00:57:16,620
they probably won't remind you that you have it.

1239
00:57:16,620 --> 00:57:20,620
And then you can buy Disability Insurance.

1240
00:57:20,620 --> 00:57:24,120
But part of the problem with private offering

1241
00:57:24,120 --> 00:57:27,620
of these insurances, Disability Insurance, if it's offered privately,

1242
00:57:27,620 --> 00:57:29,620
is that it's not going to be a disability.

1243
00:57:29,620 --> 00:57:31,620
It's going to be a selection bias problem.

1244
00:57:31,620 --> 00:57:35,620
That some people who realize that they are going

1245
00:57:35,620 --> 00:57:39,620
to have a disability will buy the insurance right away.

1246
00:57:39,620 --> 00:57:45,620
And then we have health insurance.

1247
00:57:45,620 --> 00:57:49,120
I don't want to use the U.S. as an example for

1248
00:57:49,120 --> 00:57:52,620
this, because we're kind of a laggard on most of these things.

1249
00:57:52,620 --> 00:57:54,620
But we had a new health

1250
00:57:54,620 --> 00:57:56,620
bill that passed under Obama last year.

1251
00:57:56,620 --> 00:57:58,620
And it's starting to gear up.

1252
00:57:58,620 --> 00:58:01,120
But the U.S. is not

1253
00:58:01,120 --> 00:58:03,620
the world leader in health insurance.

1254
00:58:03,620 --> 00:58:07,120
But it has currently about 40

1255
00:58:07,120 --> 00:58:10,620
million people with no health insurance.

1256
00:58:10,620 --> 00:58:19,620
And then there's workers' compensation.

1257
00:58:19,620 --> 00:58:24,620
These are the main aspects of social insurance today.

1258
00:58:24,620 --> 00:58:26,620
Workers' compensation is an older form of health insurance.

1259
00:58:26,620 --> 00:58:29,620
That preceded health insurance.

1260
00:58:29,620 --> 00:58:31,620
At least in the United States.

1261
00:58:31,620 --> 00:58:35,620
And it compensates workers for

1262
00:58:35,620 --> 00:58:39,620
accidents that occurred at work.

1263
00:58:39,620 --> 00:58:42,120
And in the U.S., this

1264
00:58:42,120 --> 00:58:44,620
came in in the Progressive Era.

1265
00:58:44,620 --> 00:58:48,120
But all these are risk management

1266
00:58:48,120 --> 00:58:51,620
devices that are offered by governments.

1267
00:58:51,620 --> 00:58:56,620
Now, these social insurance schemes are really important.

1268
00:58:56,620 --> 00:58:59,620
They're relatively recent innovations.

1269
00:58:59,620 --> 00:59:09,120
And in fact, I think that the first national, I know that, the

1270
00:59:09,120 --> 00:59:18,620
first national social insurance programs began in Germany in the late 19th century.

1271
00:59:18,620 --> 00:59:22,120
So, another theme of this course has been

1272
00:59:22,120 --> 00:59:25,620
that the ability to do things like this,

1273
00:59:26,620 --> 00:59:31,620
requires a developed society with a certain technology.

1274
00:59:31,620 --> 00:59:34,120
With an information technology,

1275
00:59:34,120 --> 00:59:36,620
with a bureaucratic technology.

1276
00:59:36,620 --> 00:59:39,620
And they haven't been around for that long.

1277
00:59:39,620 --> 00:59:43,620
If you go back in history, 200, 300

1278
00:59:43,620 --> 00:59:47,620
or more years, there wasn't social insurance anywhere.

1279
00:59:47,620 --> 00:59:51,620
Maybe there was in some isolated community.

1280
00:59:51,620 --> 00:59:54,620
But basically, it didn't exist.

1281
00:59:54,620 --> 00:59:59,620
And so, the history of social insurance is interesting.

1282
00:59:59,620 --> 01:00:04,620
And I think it mimics,

1283
01:00:04,620 --> 01:00:09,620
it reflects information technology growth.

1284
01:00:09,620 --> 01:00:14,620
So, the country that is most remarkable

1285
01:00:14,620 --> 01:00:19,620
for having invented social insurance is Germany.

1286
01:00:19,620 --> 01:00:23,620
Under Otto von Bismarck.

1287
01:00:23,620 --> 01:00:26,620
In the 1880s.

1288
01:00:26,620 --> 01:00:31,120
And I think that it happened

1289
01:00:31,120 --> 01:00:35,620
there first, because of the information technology.

1290
01:00:35,620 --> 01:00:44,620
But it's an interesting story.

1291
01:00:44,620 --> 01:00:48,620
There were various attempts to start social

1292
01:00:48,620 --> 01:00:52,620
insurance in different parts of the world,

1293
01:00:52,620 --> 01:00:54,620
that were half-hearted and failed.

1294
01:00:54,620 --> 01:00:58,120
In the United Kingdom, in the late

1295
01:00:58,120 --> 01:01:01,620
1700s, there was a town called Spanumland.

1296
01:01:01,620 --> 01:01:04,620
A tiny town in the UK.

1297
01:01:04,620 --> 01:01:07,620
That decided they would start social insurance.

1298
01:01:07,620 --> 01:01:10,120
And they decided that there was a living

1299
01:01:10,120 --> 01:01:12,620
wage that anyone ought to be able to earn.

1300
01:01:12,620 --> 01:01:14,620
And anyone who earned less than that,

1301
01:01:14,620 --> 01:01:16,620
would have the difference paid by the town.

1302
01:01:16,620 --> 01:01:20,620
So, the town, it's called the Spanumland Law.

1303
01:01:20,620 --> 01:01:21,620
And that town was called Spanumland.

1304
01:01:21,620 --> 01:01:26,620
And that town offered to pay the difference.

1305
01:01:26,620 --> 01:01:28,620
The problem was, it didn't work.

1306
01:01:28,620 --> 01:01:32,620
Too many people reported that they were getting

1307
01:01:32,620 --> 01:01:36,620
less income than the living income that they defined.

1308
01:01:36,620 --> 01:01:43,620
And the town discovered it was being ripped off.

1309
01:01:43,620 --> 01:01:46,620
And so, they couldn't, the problem is, they

1310
01:01:46,620 --> 01:01:49,620
couldn't identify what a person's income really was accurately.

1311
01:01:49,620 --> 01:01:52,620
They couldn't tell whether someone was goofing off or not.

1312
01:01:52,620 --> 01:01:56,620
And so, they dropped it.

1313
01:01:56,620 --> 01:01:59,620
It was, I think there are

1314
01:01:59,620 --> 01:02:02,620
other examples of failed experiments like this.

1315
01:02:02,620 --> 01:02:11,620
But, it took hold first in Germany in the 1880s.

1316
01:02:11,620 --> 01:02:18,620
And they got accident insurance.

1317
01:02:19,620 --> 01:02:22,620
Health insurance.

1318
01:02:22,620 --> 01:02:31,620
And old age insurance, or retirement.

1319
01:02:31,620 --> 01:02:35,620
For everyone in Germany in the 1880s.

1320
01:02:35,620 --> 01:02:38,120
And it was considered a fiasco

1321
01:02:38,120 --> 01:02:40,620
by other countries of the world.

1322
01:02:40,620 --> 01:02:44,120
When Germany announced these plans to create social

1323
01:02:44,120 --> 01:02:47,620
insurance, the London Times wrote an article saying,

1324
01:02:47,620 --> 01:02:48,620
this whole thing is a fiasco.

1325
01:02:48,620 --> 01:02:51,620
This whole thing will fall through and fail.

1326
01:02:51,620 --> 01:02:57,620
The government is going to run all of these insurance policies for something like, I

1327
01:02:57,620 --> 01:03:03,620
don't know how many, I think there were 11 million workers in Germany at the time.

1328
01:03:03,620 --> 01:03:06,620
And in London they said, you've got to be kidding.

1329
01:03:06,620 --> 01:03:09,120
No government in the world has

1330
01:03:09,120 --> 01:03:11,620
ever managed to run something like this.

1331
01:03:11,620 --> 01:03:16,620
Let's think particularly about retirement insurance.

1332
01:03:16,620 --> 01:03:22,120
The German government set up a plan whereby people would

1333
01:03:22,120 --> 01:03:27,620
contribute over their working lives to the Social Security system.

1334
01:03:27,620 --> 01:03:31,620
And the system would then, years later, 30, 40

1335
01:03:31,620 --> 01:03:35,620
years later, keep a tab about how much they'd contributed.

1336
01:03:35,620 --> 01:03:37,120
And then pay them a pension

1337
01:03:37,120 --> 01:03:38,620
for the rest of their lives.

1338
01:03:38,620 --> 01:03:41,620
So, the Times wondered aloud, are

1339
01:03:41,620 --> 01:03:44,620
they going to mess this up?

1340
01:03:44,620 --> 01:03:45,620
They've got to keep records for 40 years.

1341
01:03:45,620 --> 01:03:48,620
They were talking about the government keeping records.

1342
01:03:48,620 --> 01:03:52,620
And they thought nobody can really manage to

1343
01:03:52,620 --> 01:03:56,620
do this, and that it will collapse and ruin.

1344
01:03:56,620 --> 01:03:58,620
But it didn't.

1345
01:03:58,620 --> 01:04:02,120
The Germans managed to do this

1346
01:04:02,120 --> 01:04:05,620
in the 1880s for the first time.

1347
01:04:05,620 --> 01:04:08,620
And actually, it was an idea

1348
01:04:08,620 --> 01:04:11,620
that was copied all over the world.

1349
01:04:11,620 --> 01:04:12,620
So, why is it that Germany

1350
01:04:12,620 --> 01:04:13,620
was able to do something like this?

1351
01:04:13,620 --> 01:04:14,620
Why?

1352
01:04:14,620 --> 01:04:16,620
Because it was a very important thing.

1353
01:04:16,620 --> 01:04:18,620
It was a very important thing.

1354
01:04:18,620 --> 01:04:22,120
And it was a very important thing for the German government to be able

1355
01:04:22,120 --> 01:04:25,620
to do something like this in the 1880s, when it was not doable anywhere else.

1356
01:04:25,620 --> 01:04:27,620
It had never been done until that time.

1357
01:04:27,620 --> 01:04:33,620
I think this has to do ultimately with technology.

1358
01:04:33,620 --> 01:04:38,120
And technology, particularly information technology, was

1359
01:04:38,120 --> 01:04:42,620
advancing rapidly in the 19th century.

1360
01:04:42,620 --> 01:04:49,120
So, what happened in Europe that made

1361
01:04:49,120 --> 01:04:55,620
it possible to institute these radical new ideas?

1362
01:04:55,620 --> 01:05:00,620
So, I just give a list of one thing.

1363
01:05:00,620 --> 01:05:03,620
Paper.

1364
01:05:03,620 --> 01:05:05,620
This is information technology, but you don't think.

1365
01:05:05,620 --> 01:05:08,620
In the 18th century, paper,

1366
01:05:08,620 --> 01:05:11,620
ordinary paper, was very expensive.

1367
01:05:11,620 --> 01:05:14,620
Because it was made from cloth in those days.

1368
01:05:14,620 --> 01:05:17,620
They didn't know how to make paper from wood.

1369
01:05:17,620 --> 01:05:20,620
And it had to be handmade.

1370
01:05:20,620 --> 01:05:24,620
As a result, if you bought a newspaper

1371
01:05:24,620 --> 01:05:28,620
in, say, 1790, it would be just one page.

1372
01:05:28,620 --> 01:05:31,620
And it would be printed on the smallest print.

1373
01:05:31,620 --> 01:05:33,620
Because it was just so expensive.

1374
01:05:33,620 --> 01:05:36,120
It would cost you like $20

1375
01:05:36,120 --> 01:05:38,620
in today's prices to buy one newspaper.

1376
01:05:38,620 --> 01:05:40,620
Then they invented the paper machine.

1377
01:05:40,620 --> 01:05:42,620
That made it mechanically.

1378
01:05:42,620 --> 01:05:44,620
And they made it out of wood pulp.

1379
01:05:44,620 --> 01:05:47,620
And suddenly, the cost of paper went down.

1380
01:05:47,620 --> 01:05:50,620
By the way, I found an

1381
01:05:50,620 --> 01:05:53,620
old textbook from Yale University from 1837.

1382
01:05:53,620 --> 01:05:57,620
It's still sitting there on the shelf in the library.

1383
01:05:57,620 --> 01:05:58,620
I was looking.

1384
01:05:58,620 --> 01:06:01,620
We were using a Principles of

1385
01:06:01,620 --> 01:06:04,620
Economics textbook for all Yale students.

1386
01:06:04,620 --> 01:06:06,620
They all had to take the same course.

1387
01:06:06,620 --> 01:06:07,620
And it was a nice little book.

1388
01:06:07,620 --> 01:06:08,620
But it was so small.

1389
01:06:08,620 --> 01:06:09,620
I picked it up.

1390
01:06:09,620 --> 01:06:10,620
I mean, it was so small.

1391
01:06:10,620 --> 01:06:12,620
It was like that big.

1392
01:06:12,620 --> 01:06:14,620
You could put it in your pocket.

1393
01:06:14,620 --> 01:06:17,620
I think it's because books were just expensive.

1394
01:06:17,620 --> 01:06:20,620
You have these huge textbooks now, right?

1395
01:06:20,620 --> 01:06:21,620
They weigh so much.

1396
01:06:21,620 --> 01:06:24,620
They probably challenge your back.

1397
01:06:24,620 --> 01:06:26,620
Back then, you could carry your books.

1398
01:06:26,620 --> 01:06:29,620
There was a fundamental economic difference.

1399
01:06:29,620 --> 01:06:32,620
And so, paper was one of the things.

1400
01:06:32,620 --> 01:06:34,620
And you never got a receipt for anything.

1401
01:06:34,620 --> 01:06:36,120
When you bought something, you go to a store

1402
01:06:36,120 --> 01:06:37,620
and buy something, you think you'd get a receipt?

1403
01:06:37,620 --> 01:06:39,620
Absolutely not.

1404
01:06:39,620 --> 01:06:41,620
You would buy a copy of something.

1405
01:06:41,620 --> 01:06:43,620
You would buy a copy of something.

1406
01:06:43,620 --> 01:06:45,620
Well, they wouldn't know why.

1407
01:06:45,620 --> 01:06:47,620
But that's the ultimate reason.

1408
01:06:47,620 --> 01:06:49,620
Too expensive.

1409
01:06:49,620 --> 01:06:52,620
So, they invented paper, too.

1410
01:06:52,620 --> 01:06:54,620
Carbon paper.

1411
01:06:54,620 --> 01:06:57,620
Do you people even know what this is?

1412
01:06:57,620 --> 01:07:00,620
Anyone here heard of carbon paper?

1413
01:07:00,620 --> 01:07:01,620
Maybe.

1414
01:07:01,620 --> 01:07:02,620
I don't know.

1415
01:07:02,620 --> 01:07:05,120
It used to be that when you wanted to make

1416
01:07:05,120 --> 01:07:07,620
a copy of something, you didn't have any copying machines,

1417
01:07:07,620 --> 01:07:09,620
you just had to write on it.

1418
01:07:09,620 --> 01:07:10,620
I don't know if I

1419
01:07:10,620 --> 01:07:11,620
have to explain this to you.

1420
01:07:11,620 --> 01:07:13,620
You know what carbon paper is?

1421
01:07:13,620 --> 01:07:15,620
You put it between two sheets of paper, and you write

1422
01:07:15,620 --> 01:07:17,620
on the upper one, and it comes through on the lower one.

1423
01:07:17,620 --> 01:07:20,620
This was never invented until the 19th century.

1424
01:07:20,620 --> 01:07:22,620
Nobody had carbon paper.

1425
01:07:22,620 --> 01:07:24,620
You couldn't make copies of any.

1426
01:07:24,620 --> 01:07:26,620
There was no way to make a copy.

1427
01:07:26,620 --> 01:07:29,620
They hadn't invented photography yet.

1428
01:07:29,620 --> 01:07:32,620
They had no way to make a copy.

1429
01:07:32,620 --> 01:07:35,620
You had to just hand copy everything.

1430
01:07:35,620 --> 01:07:36,620
And so,

1431
01:07:37,620 --> 01:07:39,620
the first copying machine, maybe I mentioned

1432
01:07:39,620 --> 01:07:41,620
that, didn't come until the 20th century.

1433
01:07:41,620 --> 01:07:44,620
And they were photographic.

1434
01:07:44,620 --> 01:07:48,620
And the typewriter.

1435
01:07:48,620 --> 01:07:51,620
That was invented in the 1870s.

1436
01:07:51,620 --> 01:07:55,620
Now, it may seem like a small thing, but it

1437
01:07:55,620 --> 01:07:59,620
was a very important thing, because you could make accurate documents.

1438
01:07:59,620 --> 01:08:01,620
And they were not subject

1439
01:08:01,620 --> 01:08:03,620
to misinterpretation because of sloppy handwriting.

1440
01:08:03,620 --> 01:08:05,620
I'm doing sloppy handwriting here for you.

1441
01:08:05,620 --> 01:08:06,620
But,

1442
01:08:07,620 --> 01:08:10,620
I'm using the old technology here.

1443
01:08:10,620 --> 01:08:11,620
But,

1444
01:08:11,620 --> 01:08:14,620
the typewriter, and you could also make many copies.

1445
01:08:14,620 --> 01:08:17,620
You could make six copies at once with carbon paper.

1446
01:08:17,620 --> 01:08:19,620
And they're all exactly the same.

1447
01:08:19,620 --> 01:08:21,120
You can file them in a different,

1448
01:08:21,120 --> 01:08:22,620
each one in a different filing cabinet.

1449
01:08:22,620 --> 01:08:27,620
For standardized forms,

1450
01:08:27,620 --> 01:08:29,620
these are forms that had fill

1451
01:08:29,620 --> 01:08:31,620
in the blank with the typewriter.

1452
01:08:31,620 --> 01:08:33,620
They had filing cabinets.

1453
01:08:37,620 --> 01:08:39,620
And finally,

1454
01:08:39,620 --> 01:08:40,620
bureaucracy developed.

1455
01:08:40,620 --> 01:08:43,620
They had management school, particularly in Germany.

1456
01:08:43,620 --> 01:08:46,120
It was famous for its

1457
01:08:46,120 --> 01:08:48,620
management schools and its business schools.

1458
01:08:48,620 --> 01:08:50,620
So, you had,

1459
01:08:50,620 --> 01:08:55,620
oh, I should add also postal service.

1460
01:08:55,620 --> 01:09:00,620
If you wanted to mail a letter in 1790,

1461
01:09:00,620 --> 01:09:01,620
you'd have trouble.

1462
01:09:01,620 --> 01:09:03,620
And it would cost you a lot.

1463
01:09:03,620 --> 01:09:06,620
Most people in 1790 got maybe one letter a year,

1464
01:09:06,620 --> 01:09:08,620
or two letters a year.

1465
01:09:08,620 --> 01:09:09,620
That was it.

1466
01:09:09,620 --> 01:09:10,620
But,

1467
01:09:10,620 --> 01:09:12,620
in the 19th century, they started setting

1468
01:09:12,620 --> 01:09:14,620
up post offices all over the world.

1469
01:09:14,620 --> 01:09:17,120
And the Germans were particularly good

1470
01:09:17,120 --> 01:09:19,620
at this kind of bureaucratic thing.

1471
01:09:19,620 --> 01:09:21,620
So, there were post offices in every town.

1472
01:09:21,620 --> 01:09:23,620
And the Social Security system

1473
01:09:23,620 --> 01:09:25,620
operated through the post offices.

1474
01:09:25,620 --> 01:09:27,620
Because once you have post offices in every town,

1475
01:09:27,620 --> 01:09:31,620
you would go to make your payments on Social Security

1476
01:09:31,620 --> 01:09:32,620
at the post office.

1477
01:09:32,620 --> 01:09:34,120
And they would give you stamps,

1478
01:09:34,120 --> 01:09:35,620
and you'd paste them on a card.

1479
01:09:35,620 --> 01:09:36,620
And that's what they did.

1480
01:09:36,620 --> 01:09:43,620
And that's how you could show that you had paid.

1481
01:09:43,620 --> 01:09:48,620
So, I think that this kind of information technology

1482
01:09:48,620 --> 01:09:52,620
brought us the Social Security system.

1483
01:09:52,620 --> 01:09:56,120
And the kinds of advances in

1484
01:09:56,120 --> 01:09:59,620
information technology that we've seen more recently

1485
01:09:59,620 --> 01:10:02,620
will eventually lead to changes in the system.

1486
01:10:02,620 --> 01:10:05,620
Ultimately, technology drives finance.

1487
01:10:05,620 --> 01:10:15,620
And the system responds to changes in technology.

1488
01:10:15,620 --> 01:10:20,620
All right.

1489
01:10:20,620 --> 01:10:22,620
So, I just wanted to just wrap up.

1490
01:10:22,620 --> 01:10:27,120
I talked today about a broader

1491
01:10:27,120 --> 01:10:31,620
social purpose that's served by our finance.

1492
01:10:31,620 --> 01:10:34,620
And it takes place in terms of for-profit.

1493
01:10:35,620 --> 01:10:37,620
As well as non-profit.

1494
01:10:37,620 --> 01:10:40,120
But the distinction between for-profit

1495
01:10:40,120 --> 01:10:42,620
and non-profit is a subtle one.

1496
01:10:42,620 --> 01:10:46,620
I think what happens is, people think of a purpose,

1497
01:10:46,620 --> 01:10:48,620
of something they want to achieve.

1498
01:10:48,620 --> 01:10:51,120
And then they think, how

1499
01:10:51,120 --> 01:10:53,620
can I use our financial technology

1500
01:10:53,620 --> 01:10:58,620
to incentivize people to subscribe capital to this project,

1501
01:10:58,620 --> 01:11:02,620
or to give their personal attention to this project.

1502
01:11:02,620 --> 01:11:04,620
And then we have,

1503
01:11:04,620 --> 01:11:07,620
our system of finance has a lot of things,

1504
01:11:07,620 --> 01:11:11,120
a lot of devices that

1505
01:11:11,120 --> 01:11:14,620
can allow the provenance of capital.

1506
01:11:14,620 --> 01:11:19,620
And that allow, and that will incentivize people.

1507
01:11:19,620 --> 01:11:25,620
So, I think another theme of

1508
01:11:25,620 --> 01:11:31,620
this course has been behavioral finance.

1509
01:11:31,620 --> 01:11:33,620
That I think people are psychological.

1510
01:11:33,620 --> 01:11:35,620
They have emotions.

1511
01:11:35,620 --> 01:11:40,620
And they react in complicated ways.

1512
01:11:40,620 --> 01:11:43,620
One of the oldest themes of

1513
01:11:43,620 --> 01:11:46,620
economics is that people respond to incentives.

1514
01:11:46,620 --> 01:11:52,620
But they respond to, please to their morality as well,

1515
01:11:52,620 --> 01:11:55,620
or their ideals as well.

1516
01:11:55,620 --> 01:11:57,620
When we talk about non-profit sector,

1517
01:11:57,620 --> 01:12:02,620
it seems that the financial arrangements reflecting,

1518
01:12:02,620 --> 01:12:08,620
the combination of selfish money oriented incentives,

1519
01:12:08,620 --> 01:12:12,620
and more social purpose incentives.

1520
01:12:12,620 --> 01:12:15,620
And the same thing goes with government activities.

1521
